    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine which functions have inverses, we need to check if each function is one-to-one (injective), meaning that each input \\(x\\) corresponds to exactly one output, and that the function is onto (surjective), meaning that every output value has at least one corresponding input value. \n\nLet's analyze each function graphically:\n\n1. **Graph A**: This graph passes the vertical line test except at \\(x = -2\\) and \\(x = 0\\), where it \"jumps\" to the right. This indicates a lack of one-to-one correspondence; therefore, \\(A\\) does not have an inverse.\n\n2. **Graph B**: This graph is a quadratic function with one distinct maximum point at \\(y = -3\\) when \\(x = -2\\) and no vertical stretching. The graph does not extend infinitely in either direction, indicating the function is not onto. Therefore, \\(B\\) does not have an inverse.\n\n3. **Graph C**: This graph also does not pass the vertical line test with open intervals and does not extend infinitely in either direction. Therefore, \\(C\\) does not have an inverse.\n\n4. **Graph D**: This graph slices a horizontal line through the \"jumps\" and is open on both sides but does not extend infinitely in either direction. Therefore, \\(D\\) does not have an inverse.\n\n5. **Graph E**: This graph is a cubic function which has one \"thick\" bend (a cusp) at \\(x = -2\\). A cubic function can be flipped around its axis of symmetry (a vertical line through the cusp), yielding an inverse function where the cusp becomes undefined. Therefore, \\(E\\) has an inverse.\n\nBased on the analysis, the only graph that passes both tests of being one-to-one and onto is Graph E. So, the answer is \\( \\boxed{E} \\)",
