    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine which of the given functions have inverses, we need to check if each function is one-to-one. A function is one-to-one if every horizontal line intersects its graph at most once. This means that for each output value, there should be exactly one input value.\n\nLet's analyze each function:\n\n1. **Function A**: The graph of this function is a simple parabola opening upwards. It is not one-to-one because any vertical line will intersect the graph at both positive and negative points. Therefore, it does not have an inverse.\n\n2. **Function B**: The graph of this function consists of a line segment and a portion of a parabola. While the line segment is not a problem, the parabola opens upwards and covers both positive and negative values near its vertex, indicating that it is not one-to-one. Thus, it does not have an inverse.\n\n3. **Function C**: This function is a vertical line, which is one-to-one. Any vertical line will only intersect the graph at one point. This means the function is one-to-one and has an inverse.\n\n4. **Function D**: The graph of this function consists of a parabola and an arc. The parabola opens upwards, but the arc could potentially cause it to skip \\(y\\)-values, making it not one-to-one. Therefore, it does not have an inverse.\n\n5. **Function E**: This function is a smooth curve (a portion of a parabola) closed to a point. Like Function D, it could potentially have missing \\(y\\)-values, making it not one-to-one. Therefore, it does not have an inverse.\n\nBased on the analysis, only Function C is one-to-one because it is a vertical line.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \"C\".",
